+# wld01 capacity incident — root cause
+
+**Incident window:** 2026-03-10T23:52:14Z through 2026-03-11T06:00:00Z
+**Affected object:** `wld01-vsan-ds01` (VMWARE / Datastore)
+**Evidence:** pulled with `src/VcfOpsClient.java` against the VCF Operations 9.1 suite-api.
+Every figure below is quoted from a response, not from recollection.
+
+## Summary
+
+Two independent things went wrong, and only one of them is a storage problem.
+
+1. `wld01-vsan-ds01` genuinely ran out of allocatable capacity, and VCF Operations
+   detected it on time and raised a CRITICAL alert at 2026-03-10T23:52:14Z.
+2. Nobody was paged, because the outbound notification task that should have delivered
+   that alert failed with an SMTP connect timeout and silently dropped the notifications.
+
+The popular theory before the evidence was pulled — "Operations stopped collecting from
+the vCenter adapter, so we were flying blind" — is wrong and is contradicted below.
+
+## How the evidence was gathered
+
+| Step | operationId | What it answered |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| 1 | `acquireToken` | Opened a suite-api session (no `authSource` supplied, so the field was not sent). |
+| 2 | `getMatchingResources` | Resolved the name `wld01-vsan-ds01` to its resource UUID and, incidentally, its collection status. |
+| 3 | `queryAlert` | Listed the active CRITICAL/IMMEDIATE alerts scoped to that resource UUID. |
+| 4 | `getAlertContributingSymptoms` | Named the symptoms that actually triggered the alert. |
+| 5 | `getSymptoms` | Supplied the human-readable message, `statKey` and timestamps for those symptoms. |
+| 6 | `getTasksStatus` | Listed the tasks in `ERROR`, which is where the notification failure surfaced. |
+| 7 | `releaseToken` | Closed the session. |
+
+Step 4 is required because `contributing-symptom` carries only `symptomId` and
+`symptomDefinitionsIds`; the message and the statKey live on the `symptom` objects that
+step 5 returns. Neither step alone identifies what breached.
+
+## What actually broke
+
+`queryAlert` returned exactly one active alert on the datastore:
+
+- alert definition **"Datastore is running out of disk space"**
+  (`AlertDefinition-VMWARE-Datastore-capacity-remaining`)
+- `alertLevel` CRITICAL, `status` ACTIVE, `controlState` OPEN
+- `startTimeUTC` 1773186734000 = 2026-03-10T23:52:14Z, last updated 2026-03-11T03:10:22Z
+
+`getAlertContributingSymptoms` attributed it to two symptoms in `capacity-symptom-set`,
+and `getSymptoms` filled them in:
+
+| statKey | Criticality | Started | Message |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `diskspace|used_pct` | CRITICAL | 2026-03-10T23:52:14Z | Datastore space used is 97.4% of 40.00 TiB usable (critical threshold 90%, warning threshold 80%) |
+| `diskspace|freespace` | IMMEDIATE | 2026-03-11T02:32:41Z | Allocatable free space on wld01-vsan-ds01 is 118 GB once the vSAN resync slack reserve is held back; new VM provisioning is being refused |
+
+So the capacity breach crossed the critical threshold at 23:52Z, and the second-order
+effect — provisioning refusals, with only 118 GB allocatable — landed 2h40m later at
+02:32:41Z. That is the failure users felt.
+
+## Why nobody was paged
+
+`getTasksStatus` filtered to `taskState=ERROR` returned two failed tasks. One is
+unrelated noise ("Management pack content synchronisation", content repository
+`packs.example.com` returning HTTP 503). The other is the cause of the silence:
+
+> **Outbound notification delivery for alert plugin 'ops-smtp-relay'** — ERROR,
+> "Delivery aborted after 5 retries", last updated 2026-03-11T03:10:22Z
+>
+> - `ops-smtp-relay: connect timed out to smtp.wld01.example.com:587 after 30000 ms`
+> - `ops-smtp-relay: 3 notification(s) dropped after 5 retries, alertId=<the capacity alert above>`
+> - `ops-smtp-relay: outbound plugin marked unhealthy at 2026-03-11T03:10:22Z`
+
+The second error message carries the alert UUID of the capacity alert, so the dropped
+notifications are the ones for this incident. The alert existed in the platform the whole
+time; it just never left it.
+
+## What the evidence rules out
+
+Monitoring was never interrupted. `getMatchingResources` returned, for the datastore's
+only adapter instance, `resourceState` **STARTED** and `resourceStatus`
+**DATA_RECEIVING**, with `statusMessage` "Collecting; last collection completed
+2026-03-11T06:00:00Z". The object was also at `resourceHealth` RED with
+`resourceHealthValue` 8.0, i.e. the platform was scoring it correctly. There is no
+collection gap to explain, and no adapter to restart.
